WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.590
In one night, I sent six waves of AI
coding agents across the whole platform.

2
00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:11.093
Each wave had a job: speed,
bugs, dead code, or structure.

3
00:00:11.493 --> 00:00:16.033
Agents write code fast, so every
change had to pass the same rules.

4
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:19.902
Measure first, with the same
harness before and after.

5
00:00:20.202 --> 00:00:23.307
At least five runs, with
the median and the spread.

6
00:00:23.707 --> 00:00:28.322
The outputs must be identical, checked
against saved fixtures and hashes.

7
00:00:28.722 --> 00:00:32.311
Every bug fix comes with a test
that fails on the old code.

8
00:00:32.711 --> 00:00:35.075
Production is only read, never written.

9
00:00:35.375 --> 00:00:39.363
And everything lands on a preview
branch, for a person to merge.

10
00:00:39.963 --> 00:00:41.484
Here's some of what came back.

11
00:00:41.784 --> 00:00:46.969
File downloads went from
837 milliseconds to 45.

12
00:00:47.369 --> 00:00:53.624
Time lost to memory clean-up fell
from 27.5 percent of a run to 9.3.

13
00:00:54.024 --> 00:01:01.506
The home page on a phone shows its main
content in 1.98 seconds, down from 3.26.

14
00:01:01.906 --> 00:01:04.008
Security bugs were found and fixed.

15
00:01:04.308 --> 00:01:07.903
And the build now fails if any
endpoint has no access check.
